hin chain so it calculates  this factor called the potential scale  reduction factor this guy here so it's  the essentially the ratio of the overall  variance over the within chain variance  and over time the between chain variance  should go away so as n gets large and  being the number of samples this goes  away and it's just W versus so those  should be approximately one so you're  looking for they call our hat values of  near one and so you can calculate that  pull in the R hat from our V's and  calculate them you can see in here most  of these are around one you start  worrying if it's you know 0.8 or less  something like that and in fact if you  plot some of the so there's a forest  plot plot forest function they're really  good for visualizing posteriors of  lots of variables in the same figure so  these are posterior plots of the  standard deviation of the and standard  deviation of the slope and intercept and  if you say our hat equals true it will  plot the our hat values for you okay and  you can see both of these are close to  one okay autocorrelation is another nice  diagnostic autocorrelation isn't always  a huge deal you can expect some  autocorrelation because these are after  all Markov chains they're dependent  samples but you do want to minimize it  because what it will do is essentially  reduce if you have a high level of  autocorrelation it reduces the  independence between draws and  essentially your effective sample size  goes down so you have to draw more  samples to get the same level of  precision from your estimates so you can  plot an autocorrelation function to look  at this so these are two plots of MA and  actually same plot of MA one for each  chain and you can see here the each each  value on the X is a lag so the first  notch is the lag between one step lag a  two step lag and so so on all the way  out to a hundred and so it should look  like this sort of random small amounts  of random variation what you're looking  to avoid are things like this so you  here we have particular in the second  chain a high level of autocorrelation  all the way up to you know lag 20 or 30  and it typically means you got to Reaper  ammeter eyes your model you don't have a  very efficient parameterization or  better tune your model and and yeah the  main consequence to this is that you  will have a lower effective sample size  so here's effective sample size which is  a function of the number of drawers that  you take and the number of chains but  you can see here's the autocorrelation  summed over all of the lags and if  that's really high it lowers the  effective sample size and you can  similarly plot make a forest plot with  ESS equals true so effective sample size  equals true and it will plot ESS instead  of our hat  so in this case I drew 2,000 samples  after burnin and you can see for these  two parameters it was pretty close to  2,000 but for the standard deviation it  was had an effective sample size that  was about only about half so we didn't  have some autocorrelation there's a  temptation if you read some early MCMC  literature and certainly papers people  published they'll thin their sample  they'll just take every tenth draw but  there's really no point in doing that  because you're what are you doing there  you're reducing your sample size by a  factor of 10 or whatever so just leave  it alone and show your autocorrelation  the effect will be the same so I've only  got a couple minutes left the only  section that I haven't been able to get  to is the gradient based sampler  Diagnostics I'll just let you do that  you know on your own it's just a  different set of Diagnostics that uses a  little bit more information because of  the fact that we had gradient  information so you can actually do a  better job of diagnosing gradient based  sampling than just metropolis Hastings  and yeah so I'll stop there and I  probably have two or three minutes left  for questions and I'm happy to answer  everyone's 